# How Do I Build with Wattle and Daub Construction?

## Short Answer

Wattle and daub is one of the oldest natural building methods in human history, combining a woven lattice of flexible sticks (the wattle) with a sticky clay-and-straw plaster (the daub).
It produces lightweight, well-insulated walls with excellent thermal performance and is ideally suited for mild and temperate climates.
The technique is low-cost, uses locally available materials, and integrates beautifully with [[timber-frame-basics]] and other [[natural-building]] approaches.

## What Is Wattle and Daub?

Wattle and daub has been used for over 6,000 years across Europe, Asia, Africa, and the Americas.
The wattle is a woven lattice of sticks or split timber, typically made from flexible species like willow, hazel, or oak.
The daub is a mixture of clay subsoil, sand, straw or animal fiber, and sometimes manure, applied wet into and over the wattle.
When it dries, it forms a solid, monolithic wall panel that is both strong and breathable.
The method is documented extensively in [[wattle-and-daub-construction]] and is closely related to [[cob-building]] and other [[natural-building-techniques]].

## Materials You Will Need

Gathering the right materials before you start saves time and produces a better wall.

**For the wattle frame:**
- Flexible green sticks 1–3 cm in diameter (willow, hazel, or similar coppice material)
- Structural upright posts (timber, often oak or chestnut)
- Horizontal rails or ziggers to support the weave

**For the daub mix:**
- Clay-rich subsoil (test by rolling into a ribbon — it should hold together)
- Coarse sand or grit for strength and to reduce cracking
- Straw, hay, or animal fiber as binder
- Water to achieve a workable consistency
- Optional: cow manure for additional plasticity and microbial binding

You can source your clay on-site or from nearby excavations, following the same subsoil testing methods described in [[cob-building-basics-detailed]].

## Step-by-Step Construction Process

### 1. Build the Timber Frame

Begin with a sturdy structural frame, typically a [[timber-frame-basics]] post-and-beam system.
The frame carries the roof and structural loads; the wattle and daub fills the panels between posts.
Space vertical studs 40–60 cm apart to create panels of a manageable width for weaving.

### 2. Install the Wattle

Weave green sticks horizontally between the vertical studs, alternating in front of and behind each upright.
Pack the weave tightly — gaps in the wattle weaken the wall and allow daub to fall through.
A well-woven wattle panel should feel firm when pressed and have no loose sticks.
The weaving tradition is related to basket-making skills; see [[coppice-and-pollard-systems]] for information on sourcing sustainable stick material.

### 3. Mix the Daub

Combine clay subsoil, sand, and straw in a ratio of roughly 4:2:1 by volume.
Add water gradually and tread or mix thoroughly — many builders use their feet on a tarp.
The mix should be stiff enough to hold its shape but wet enough to press into the wattle.
Letting the daub sit overnight (a process called " souring") improves workability.

### 4. Apply the Daub

Press the daub firmly into the wattle from both sides, ensuring it penetrates through the weave.
Build up the wall in two or three coats: a rough base coat, a straightening coat, and a final finish coat.
Score each coat with a scratcher or fork to help the next layer key in.
Allow each coat to dry partially before applying the next — typically 2–5 days depending on weather.

### 5. Finish with Natural Plaster

Once the daub is fully dry, apply a breathable finish using [[earth-plaster-and-natural-finishes]] or a lime-based render.
Lime plaster is especially good for exterior walls in wet climates because it sheds water while remaining vapor-permeable.
See [[comparison-lime-plaster-vs-earthen-plaster]] for choosing the right finish.

## Sourcing and Preparing Wattle Sticks

The best wattle sticks come from [[coppice-and-pollard-systems-for-woody-biomass]] management, where trees are cut back regularly to produce long, straight, flexible poles.
[[salix-alba]] (white willow) and other Salix species are traditional favorites because of their flexibility and rapid regrowth.
[[permaculture-willow-guide]] describes how to establish and manage willow beds specifically for craft and building material.
Hazel (Corylus species such as [[corylus-avellana]]) is another excellent choice, widely used in European timber-frame infill panels.
Freshly cut green material is easiest to weave; dried sticks become brittle and prone to breaking.

## Mixing and Applying Daub

The ideal daub depends on your local clay — some clays are very sticky and need more sand, others are sandy and need more fiber.
Always do a small test panel first and let it dry to check for cracking before mixing a large batch.
The straw acts as reinforcement, distributing stresses across the wall as it dries and settles.
Cow or horse manure has been traditionally added to daub for its fiber content and microbial activity, which improves the binding properties.
For detailed clay soil assessment, refer to [[natural-building-materials-guide]].

## What About Using Wattle and Daub in Wet Climates?

Wattle and daub walls are vulnerable to prolonged moisture exposure, so generous roof overhangs (at least 60 cm) are essential.
A rising damp barrier such as a stone stemwall lifts the wattle panel above splashback from ground level.
Lime-washing the exterior every few years maintains water resistance while keeping the wall breathable.
This method performs best under protective conditions — deep eaves, good drainage, and sheltered sites — similar to the climate considerations for [[straw-bale-construction]] and [[adobe-construction]].
In very rainy regions, consider combining wattle and daub infill with an outer rainscreen or cladding.

## What About Structural Engineering and Building Codes?

Wattle and daub is typically used as infill within a structural frame and does not carry vertical loads.
The timber frame handles all structural requirements, so engineering focuses on the frame itself rather than the panels.
Many jurisdictions allow owner-built structures under a certain size without full building permits, but always check local regulations.
[[natural-building]] organizations can provide guidance on code compliance for earthen construction.
Some regions (parts of the UK, France, Germany, and New Zealand) have specific heritage or natural building codes that recognize wattle and daub as a valid construction method.

## Key Points

- Wattle and daub combines a woven stick lattice with a clay-straw plaster to create solid, breathable walls.
- The technique is ideal as infill within a [[timber-frame-basics]] structural system.
- Source flexible sticks from [[coppice-and-pollard-systems]] using species like willow and hazel.
- Mix daub from local subsoil, sand, and straw — test your clay before building large panels.
- Protect walls with generous roof overhangs and finish with breathable plasters.
- The method is very low-cost and uses almost entirely site-sourced or locally available materials.
- It is one of the fastest natural building techniques for producing finished walls.
